      "expected": "answered",
      "note": "Activity 2 names the Malthusian trap. It does not define it.",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "latencyMs": 1536,
      "answer": "In this lesson, the Malthusian trap is the classical growth theory idea that population growth tends to wipe out gains in income per person: when output rises, population increases until people are pushed back to subsistence. Because land is fixed and diminishing returns set in, any temporary improvement in living standards is eventually absorbed by more mouths to feed. In your teacher's plan, it is one key mechanism used to explain why economies stayed poor for so long.",
      "basis": "explanation",
      "refusal": null,
      "hostSentRefusal": false,
